declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 14, $j$4.2 Philippians and Colossians: Joy and Christ Above All$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Philippians 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Philippians/2"}, {"title": "Philippians 4 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Philippians/4"}, {"title": "Colossians 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Colossians/1"}, {"title": "Colossians 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Colossians/3"}], "body": "Read Philippians 2, Philippians 4, Colossians 1 and Colossians 3 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nPhilippi was the first city in Europe where Paul started a church, and the Philippians loved him and supported him. Paul wrote to them from prison, yet Philippians is full of joy. In chapter 2 he described the humble mind of Christ, who became a servant and died on the cross, and whom God has highly exalted. In chapter 4 he taught believers to rejoice in the Lord, to pray about everything instead of worrying, and to trust that they can do all things through Christ who gives strength. Colossians was written to a church in Asia Minor that was in danger of false teaching. Paul answered by showing that Jesus Christ is Lord over all creation and head of the church, and he urged believers to set their hearts on things above and to work as unto the Lord.\n\nKey words: joy, humility, exalted, preeminence, thanksgiving.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell how Philippians 2 describes the humble mind of Christ. (2) Write Philippians 4:6 and 7 in your own words. (3) Explain what Colossians 1 teaches about who Jesus is.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Today we study two prison letters that fit together well. The first is Philippians, which is called the letter of joy. The second is Colossians, which shows us that Jesus Christ is above everything.\n\nPhilippi was the city where Paul and Silas sang in the jail at midnight and the jailer was saved, as we saw in Acts 16. The church there was Paul's dear friend, and they sent him gifts when he was in need. Paul wrote back from prison, and the word joy and the word rejoice appear again and again.\n\nIn chapter 2 Paul asked the Philippians to have the mind of Christ. Philippians 2:5 says, Let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus: Jesus was God, yet He took the form of a servant. Verse 8 says, And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross. Because of this, God lifted Him up. Verse 9 says, Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name: And verse 11 says, And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.\n\nChapter 4 tells us how to live with joy even in hard times. Philippians 4:4 says, Rejoice in the Lord alway: and again I say, Rejoice. Verse 6 says, Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God. Verse 7 gives the promise, And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus. Paul also wrote in verse 13, I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me. That verse was written by a man in prison who had learned to be content in every state.\n\nNow we turn to Colossians. Colossae was a small city in Asia Minor, and false teachers there were saying that Jesus was not enough. Paul answered by lifting up Christ. Colossians 1:15 says, Who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature: Verse 17 says, And he is before all things, and by him all things consist. Verse 18 says, And he is the head of the body, the church: who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence. Jesus is Creator, Lord and head of the church, and He must have first place in everything.\n\nPaul then showed what this means for daily life. Colossians 3:2 says, Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth. And verse 23 says, And whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord, and not unto men; Whether it is homework, a job or a chore, we do it for the Lord.\n\nYour task is to tell how Philippians 2 describes the humble mind of Christ, write Philippians 4:6 and 7 in your own words, and explain what Colossians 1 teaches about Jesus. Then take the four question check.\n\nJoy comes from the Lord, and He must have first place. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.2 Philippians and Colossians: Joy and Christ Above All$j$);
end
$seed$;
